## Why This Is Asked

Tests a real-world asynchronous export flow requiring idempotency, retry logic, and observability. It validates high-concurrency behavior and failure modes across the message broker and downstream analytics partner.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent POST endpoints with deduplication keys
- At-least-once vs exactly-once processing semantics
- End-to-end observability and monitoring
- Exponential backoff retry strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton for idempotent export
async function testIdempotentExport(exportId) {
  const res1 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  const res2 = await post('/exports', {
    user_id: 'user123',
    export_options: { format: 'csv', date_range: '30d' },
    export_id: exportId
  });

  // First request should queue the export
  assert(res1.status === 202);
  // Second request should return existing export
  assert(res2.status === 200 && res2.data.export_id === exportId);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for distributed transactional APIs require ensuring idempotency and exactly-once semantics across regions, handling race conditions, and resilience to partial failures. This question probes real-world test orchestration and failure-mode diagnosis.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and exactly-once processing
- Cross-region race conditions and clock skew
- Central queue backpressure and retry strategies
- End-to-end test orchestration and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test snippet using Node.js and fetch
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

async function testIdempotency() {
  const idempotencyKey = 'test-key-' + Date.now();
  const requests = Array(10).fill().map(() => 
    fetch('https://api.example.com/transactions', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'idempotency-key': idempotencyKey,
        'Content-Type': 'application/json'
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ amount: 100, to: 'account-123' })
    })
  );

  const responses = await Promise.all(requests);
  const successCount = responses.filter(r => r.ok).length;
  console.log(`Expected: 1, Actual: ${successCount}`);
}
```

## Tooling Suggestions

- **Test Coordination**: Kubernetes Jobs with shared ConfigMaps for distributed test orchestration
- **Chaos Engineering**: Gremlin or Chaos Mesh for simulating regional outages
- **Queue Testing**: LocalStack or TestContainers for queue behavior simulation
- **Monitoring**: Prometheus metrics + Grafana dashboards for real-time observation
- **Assertion Framework**: Custom test harness with distributed state verification

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design complex end-to-end tests for a streaming API with multi-tenancy, delivery guarantees, and resilience under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket streaming with per-tenant isolation and backpressure control
- Message deduplication via unique messageIds for at-least-once delivery
- Reconnection logic with exponential backoff and replay semantics
- Fault injection for broker failover and regional outages
- Latency and throughput SLA validation

## Code Example
```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const { Kafka } = require('kafkajs');
const Redis = require('ioredis');

const TENANTS = ['tenant-a', 'tenant-b', 'tenant-c'];
const SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS = 400;
const MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS = 10;
const BASE_BACKOFF_MS = 100;

class MultiTenantTest {
  constructor() {
    this.redis = new Redis();
    this.connections = new Map();
    this.messageIds = new Map();
    this.latencies = [];
  }

  async setup() {
    await this.redis.flushdb();
    for (const tenant of TENANTS) {
      await this.redis.set(`seed:${tenant}`, Math.random().toString(36));
    }
  }

  async connectTenant(tenant) {
    const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/feed');
    const clientId = `${tenant}-${Date.now()}`;

    ws.on('open', () => {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'subscribe', tenant, clientId }));
    });

    ws.on('message', (data) => {
      const msg = JSON.parse(data);
      if (msg.tenant !== tenant) {
        throw new Error(`CROSS-TENANT LEAK: ${tenant} received ${msg.tenant} data`);
      }
      const latency = Date.now() - msg.timestamp;
      this.latencies.push(latency);
      this.trackMessageId(msg.messageId, tenant);
    });

    this.connections.set(tenant, ws);
    return ws;
  }

  trackMessageId(messageId, tenant) {
    if (this.messageIds.has(messageId)) {
      const existing = this.messageIds.get(messageId);
      console.log(`DUPLICATE: ${messageId} from ${existing} to ${tenant}`);
    }
    this.messageIds.set(messageId, tenant);
  }

  async simulateBackpressure(tenant) {
    const ws = this.connections.get(tenant);
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'pause' }));
  }

  async resumeFeed(tenant) {
    const ws = this.connections.get(tenant);
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'resume' }));
  }

  async simulateBrokerOutage(region) {
    console.log(`Simulating broker outage in ${region}`);
  }

  calculatePercentile(arr, p) {
    const sorted = arr.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const idx = Math.ceil((p / 100) * sorted.length) - 1;
    return sorted[Math.max(0, idx)];
  }

  async validateSLA() {
    const p95 = this.calculatePercentile(this.latencies, 95);
    console.log(`P95 Latency: ${p95}ms`);
    if (p95 > SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS) {
      throw new Error(`SLA VIOLATION: p95 ${p95}ms exceeds ${SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS}ms`);
    }
  }
}
```

This test framework validates all key requirements: per-tenant isolation, at-least-once delivery through messageId deduplication, backpressure handling, and latency SLAs under simulated failure conditions.

